// {{sourceName}}: a starter Malloy model over {{dataPath}}, served by Publisher.
//
// Check the row count before you trust this. A spreadsheet is only a table when
// its first row is the header and every row under it is data. Plenty are not: a
// report title, a "generated on" banner, a merged group header, or a blank line
// above the header are all ordinary in an export, and none of them is visible
// from here. DuckDB takes the first row it finds as the column names and stops
// at the first blank row, so a workbook with thousands of rows can load as a
// handful, or as one, and nothing anywhere reports a problem: the package loads,
// the server says it is serving, and the query returns 200 with the wrong number.
//
// So run `overview` first and compare `record_count` against what you know is in
// the file. If it is short, the read is wrong, and the fix is below.
//
// Reading a report export properly takes two things, not one. Name the sheet and
// the range so the header is found, AND filter the decoration back out, because
// an explicit range reads every row in it: the blank spacers, the subtotal rows,
// the total, and the footnotes underneath. Pick a column every real row has and
// nothing else does, usually an id, and filter on it. Replace the source below,
// keeping the `extend` block so `overview` still exists to re-run:
//
//   source: {{sourceName}} is duckdb.sql("""
//     SELECT * FROM read_xlsx(
//       '{{dataPath}}',
//       sheet = 'Sheet1',        -- only the FIRST sheet is read unless you name one
//       header = true,
//       range = 'A5:Z100000',    -- A5 = where the real header is. EDIT BOTH BOUNDS:
//                                -- Z and 100000 are a cap, not a guess at your size.
//                                -- Columns past Z and rows past 100000 are dropped
//                                -- silently, which is the failure above all over again.
//       all_varchar = true       -- see the mixed-column note below
//     )
//     WHERE "Order ID" LIKE 'SO-%'   -- your id column, and what a real value looks like
//   """) extend {
//     measure: record_count is count()
//     view: overview is { aggregate: record_count }
//   }
//
// Both halves matter. Without the range you get the title row as your header.
// Without the filter you get the whole range, so a 1,500 row sheet reads as
// however many rows you asked for, padded with empties. Do not reach for
// `stop_at_empty`: it stops at the first blank spacer, which is usually somewhere
// in the middle of the data.
//
// `duckdb.table()` takes a plain path and nothing else, which is why every option
// above needs the SQL form. Other things a real spreadsheet does: a column mixing
// numbers and text fails at query time rather than at load, which is what
// `all_varchar = true` avoids, at the price of casting where you define measures
// (`ignore_errors = true` is the other way, nulling the bad cells); a cell someone
// typed as text, `$1,234` or `12%`, stays text even where the column looks
// numeric; dates may be text or Excel serial numbers; and every number is a
// double. `skill:malloy-gotchas-modeling` has the full list.
//
// Beyond that this is the same starting point as any seeded package: the
// scaffolder does not read your columns, so it begins with a row count. Build it
// out for your columns: group_by a column to break the count down, and define
// measures like `total_amount is amount.sum()`. Validate edits with
// malloy_compile and run new views by name with malloy_reloadPackage. The
// malloy-modeling skill walks through turning a raw table into a semantic model.

source: {{sourceName}} is duckdb.table('{{dataPath}}') extend {
  measure: record_count is count()

  view: overview is {
    aggregate: record_count
  }
}
